# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for scripts/sanitize_upgrade.sh.
#
# Only reports traced to third-party code (Minuit2, GSL, the OpenMP runtime) may be suppressed, never a report
# whose racing accesses are in AZURE2's own sources: those are findings (docs/upgrade/sanitizer_findings.md) and
# are fixed on a fix/ branch.  Each entry carries, above it, the report it silences and why that report is not an
# AZURE2 race.  Format: https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/ThreadSanitizerSuppressions

# LLVM libomp, worker suspend/resume.  A worker initializes its own suspend mutex (pthread_mutex_init in
# __kmp_suspend_initialize_thread) and the thread that wakes it locks that mutex (__kmp_resume_template); the
# order between the two is set by libomp's internal atomic flags, which TSan does not see because libomp is
# not instrumented (libarcher annotates OpenMP constructs, not the runtime's own bookkeeping).  Both stacks are
# entirely inside libomp; AZURE2 appears only as the frame that opened the parallel region.  Seen on every run
# (T1 calculation, clang 23.1.4, libomp 23.1.4, 8 threads).
race:__kmp_suspend_initialize_thread
race:__kmp_resume_template

# The same report where libomp carries no symbols (GitHub's ubuntu-24.04 runner, clang 18, libomp.so.5: its
# frames print "<null>", so the two names above match nothing).  Seen in every step of the CI's thread job,
# 2026-11-15: pthread_mutex_lock called from libomp on the main thread against pthread_mutex_init called from
# libomp on a worker, the heap block allocated by libomp; AZURE2 appears only as the frame that opened the
# parallel region (ChannelRowFill.cpp).  The entry names the interceptor of the initialization itself, anchored:
# AZURE2 never calls pthread_mutex_init (its locks are std::mutex, which has no run-time initialization), so a
# report with this frame cannot be a race between accesses of AZURE2.  A module entry (race:libomp.so) would
# hide every race inside a parallel region, whose stacks all pass through libomp, and is not used.
race:^pthread_mutex_init$
